+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/property-taxes/#article","headline":"Horry County Property Tax Rate: 4% vs 6%, With a Calculator","description":"How Horry County property taxes work: the 4% legal residence rate, the 6% rate for second homes and investment property, why the real gap is 3 to 4 times, and how to apply.","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/property-taxes/","author":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"},"reviewedBy":{"@type":"Person","name":"Timothy Nash","alternateName":"Tim Nash","jobTitle":"Broker-in-Charge"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"},"datePublished":"2026-07-19","dateModified":"2026-11-07","inLanguage":"en-US","image":"https://chapter3realty.com/og-image.jpg","mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/property-taxes/#webpage"},"about":{"@type":"Thing","name":"Horry County property taxes"},"keywords":"Horry County property taxes, Myrtle Beach property tax, 4% vs 6% South Carolina, legal residence exemption, SC property tax second home"}</script>
 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/property-taxes/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How much are property taxes in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For tax year 2026, a $400,000 home in the city of Myrtle Beach pays about $1,615 a year if it is the owner's legal residence at the 4 percent rate. The same home pays about $6,100 a year at the 6 percent rate that applies to second homes and rentals. In unincorporated Horry County the same house is taxed about $1,470 at 4 percent and about $4,800 at 6 percent. The rate class matters more than the house."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why is the gap between the 4% and 6% rate so large?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Two reasons stack. The assessment ratio, the share of your home's value that gets taxed, is 1.5 times higher. Properties at 6 percent also pay the school operating tax, 109.1 mills in Horry County for 2026, which 4 percent legal residences are fully exempt from under Act 388. Together the real bill is about 2.5 to 3.8 times higher, depending on the town."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get the 4% legal residence rate in Horry County?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Apply with the Horry County Assessor after you move in. It is never automatic; without an approved application you are billed at 6 percent. You will need your SC driver's license or ID, SC vehicle registration at the property address, and redacted federal and state tax returns, for both spouses. The county asks for applications by May 31 so your October bill prints at the right rate. One legal residence per household, and renting the home more than 72 days a year forfeits the rate."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why did my property tax bill jump the year after I bought?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Your first October bill still reflects the seller's status and the seller's taxable value. The following year, three resets can hit at once. The value resets to roughly your purchase price because a sale is an assessable transfer of interest. The ratio changes to 6 percent if the home is not your legal residence, and you lose the school operating exemption with it. That is how a $1,500 bill becomes $5,000 or more. We calculate the real post-sale number for every buyer before they offer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do retirees get a property tax break in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. On top of the 4 percent rate, homeowners 65 and older, or totally disabled or legally blind, get the Homestead Exemption: the first $50,000 of the home's market value is exempt. You need one year of South Carolina residency, and in Horry County you apply in person at the Auditor's office. It renews automatically after the first application."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I rent my home out and keep the 4% rate?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Only up to 72 days per calendar year. Rent it more than that and the property moves to 6 percent. Rent it 14 days or fewer and the rental income is also federal income tax free on top. Between 15 and 72 days you keep the 4 percent but owe accommodations taxes and need a business license."}}]}</script>
