 <section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The direct answer</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">What Carolina Forest is, and why relocating buyers choose it</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">Carolina Forest is unincorporated Horry County between US 501 and International Drive, master-planned by International Paper in the late 1990s on part of its old 30,000-acre timber tract. It is not a city and pays no city taxes; the mailing address says Myrtle Beach, ZIP 29579, and the beach is a 10-to-15-minute drive. The 2020 Census counted 23,342 residents; the latest Census estimate puts it near 26,263, and 14.5 percent of residents moved in within the past year, roughly ten points above the state's migration rate. </p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px">The draw is a combination of four things: twenty years of continuous <a href="/buyers/new-construction/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">new construction</a>, single-family prices well below beach product, no municipal millage, and simpler insurance. Insurance is standard homeowner coverage that still includes wind, because most of Carolina Forest is outside the state wind pool's territory. Most of our <a href="/buyers/relocating/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">relocating clients</a> tour it first, and many stop looking after.</p></div></section>
 
